==Latin==

===Etymology===
From {{der|la|ine-pro|*tóm}}, accusative of {{m|ine-pro|*só}}. Cf. its feminine form {{cog|la|tam}}, as in {{m|la|tamquam}}. Cognate with {{cog|grc|τότε}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA}}
* {{audio|la|la-cls-tum.ogg|Audio (Classical)}}

===Adverb===
{{la-adv|tum|-}}

# [[then]], [[thereupon]]
#: {{ux|la|'''Tum''' Caecilius in horto sedet.|'''Then''' Caecilius sits in the garden.}}
# [[at the time]], at that time, then
#: {{uxi|la|tum primum|t=for the first time, then at first}}
#: {{uxi|la|Qui '''tum''' primum allato nuntio de oppugnatione Vellaunoduni|Who '''then''' for the first time being delivered information about the siege of [[w:Vellaunodunum|Vellaunodunum]]}} ([[w:Caesar|Caesar]], [[w:de Bello Gallico|de Bello Gallico]], VII, 11)
#: {{uxi|la|Ea '''tum''' cura maxime intentos habebat Romanos, non ab ira tantum, quae in nullam unquam ciuitatem iustior fuit, quam quod urbs tam nobilis ac potens, sicut defectione sua traxerat aliquot populos, ita recepta inclinatura rursus animos uidebatur ad ueteris imperii respectum|This concern in particular troubled the mindful Romans '''at the time''', not so much because of anger, which has never been more justified against any other city, rather because a city so noble and powerful, in the same way that it had attracted the support of a number of communities by its revolt, was thought would again turn attention back towards respect for the previous government once recaptured.}} ([[w:Titus Livius|Livius]], [[w:ab urbe condita|ab urbe condita]])
# [[further]] [[on]]
#: ''...'''tum''' silvis scaena coruscis...'' - Aeneid, Book 1, Line 164

====Usage notes====
Often coupled with {{l|la|cum}}
# Such that "tum x, cum y" = "then x, when y"
#"cum x tum y"="not only x but also y"
#* {{Q|la|[[w:Titus Livius|Titus Livius]]|[[w:Ab Urbe Condita Libri|Ab Urbe Condita]]|I, 13|quote={{l|la|movet}} {{l|la|res}} '''cum''' {{l|la|multitudinem}} '''tum''' {{l|la|duces}}|t=This event '''not only''' shocked the crowd '''but also''' the commanders}}

